1.4 Goals of This Course
This course develops the foundations needed to carry out that workflow. By the end of the course, students should be able to:

translate a substantive problem into an answerable data-science question;
use Python to import, organize, explore, visualize, and model data;
explain the principles behind the methods they use and interpret their results in context;
distinguish descriptive, predictive, and causal claims;
evaluate data quality, model performance, uncertainty, and possible sources of bias;
use Git to maintain a transparent history of a project and collaborate with others;
use Quarto to combine prose, code, results, and citations in a reproducible report;
communicate findings clearly without overstating what the evidence supports; and
identify ethical concerns and take responsibility for analytical choices, including work performed with the assistance of AI.

The aim is not mastery of every available command or algorithm. Tools will change, and unfamiliar problems are unavoidable. The more durable goal is to learn how to select tools, investigate problems, verify results, and explain the reasoning that connects a question to a conclusion.

3.2 Markdown Essentials
Markdown provides the readable structure around an analysis. The following mini-report shows how headings, emphasis, a list, a labeled equation, a table, a figure, a citation, and cross-references work together:
## Assessment Results {#sec-assessment-results}

The **mean score** summarizes performance in each subject, but it does not show
the variation among students. The sample mean for subject $s$ is

$$
\bar{x}_s = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{is}.
$$ {#eq-subject-mean}

@tbl-subject-means reports the results calculated with
@eq-subject-mean.

| subject | mean score |
|:--|--:|
| data science | 78.4 |
| statistics | 72.1 |
| programming | 84.3 |

: Simulated mean scores for a class of 30 students. {#tbl-subject-means}

![Mean score by subject, with standard-error bars.](figures/scores.png){#fig-subject-means}

@fig-subject-means makes the difference among subjects easier to see.
The interpretation should also consider the variation, as explained in
@sec-assessment-results. The analysis follows reproducible-authoring
practices [@quarto2025guide].
A label in braces, such as {#eq-subject-mean}, identifies an object that Quarto should number. Use the prefixes eq-, tbl-, fig-, and sec- for equations, tables, figures, and sections. A cross-reference begins with @ and uses the matching label, as in @tbl-subject-means. During rendering, Quarto replaces that source text with the current object number. If an earlier table or figure is added, references update automatically instead of leaving a stale number in the prose.
Use a heading hierarchy rather than bold text to organize a document. Put blank lines around paragraphs, tables, and lists; write descriptive link text; keep code that should execute in a Quarto code chunk; and use meaningful captions for figures and tables. Cite sources near the claim they support. The Markdown basics reference covers tables, images, citations, cross-references, callouts, and other features.

4.3 Functions
A function should name a task, accept the data and choices needed for that task, and return a result that another part of the analysis can use. It should not quietly depend on a global variable or print a result that a later step needs to analyze. A docstring records the function’s contract, while type hints communicate the intended inputs and output; Python does not automatically enforce those hints.
Two functions can return the same answer while requiring very different amounts of computation. The Fibonacci sequence provides a small example. We use (F_0 = 0) and (F_1 = 1), so each later value is the sum of the preceding two values. A direct recursive definition is easy to read, but it repeatedly recomputes the same earlier values:

def fibonacci_recursive(n: int) -&gt; int:
    """Return the nth Fibonacci number using direct recursion."""
    if n &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError("n must be nonnegative")
    if n &lt; 2:
        return n
    return fibonacci_recursive(n - 1) + fibonacci_recursive(n - 2)

Memoization stores values that have already been computed. The functools.cache decorator supplies a general-purpose cache, so the recursive definition still mirrors the mathematics without repeating the same work:

from functools import cache


@cache
def fibonacci_memoized(n: int) -&gt; int:
    """Return the nth Fibonacci number while caching earlier results."""
    if n &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError("n must be nonnegative")
    if n &lt; 2:
        return n
    return fibonacci_memoized(n - 1) + fibonacci_memoized(n - 2)

A bottom-up dynamic-programming function builds the sequence from its base cases. It uses linear time and stores all earlier values, which is useful when the analysis needs the sequence rather than only its final value:

def fibonacci_bottom_up(n: int) -&gt; int:
    """Return the nth Fibonacci number using a table of earlier values."""
    if n &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError("n must be nonnegative")
    values = [0, 1]
    for index in range(2, n + 1):
        values.append(values[index - 1] + values[index - 2])
    return values[n] if n else values[0]

When only one value is needed, an iterative function can keep just the two values needed for the next step. It returns the same result with linear time and constant extra memory:

def fibonacci_iterative(n: int) -&gt; int:
    """Return the nth Fibonacci number by iterating from the base cases."""
    if n &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError("n must be nonnegative")
    previous, current = 0, 1
    for _ in range(n):
        previous, current = current, previous + current
    return previous


assert (
    fibonacci_recursive(10)
    == fibonacci_memoized(10)
    == fibonacci_bottom_up(10)
    == fibonacci_iterative(10)
    == 55
)

The recursive version makes the mathematical definition visible, but its repeated work grows exponentially as n increases. Memoization and the two iterative approaches reduce the repeated work to linear time. The bottom-up version uses linear extra memory, while the constant-memory version is the practical choice when only one value is needed. A small timing comparison makes the difference observable:

import timeit

def run_memoized():
    fibonacci_memoized.cache_clear()
    return fibonacci_memoized(30)


timings = {
    "recursive": timeit.timeit(lambda: fibonacci_recursive(30), number=1),
    "memoized": timeit.timeit(run_memoized, number=1),
    "bottom_up": timeit.timeit(lambda: fibonacci_bottom_up(30), number=1),
    "iterative": timeit.timeit(lambda: fibonacci_iterative(30), number=1),
}
pd.Series(timings, name="seconds")

recursive    0.186443
memoized     0.000018
bottom_up    0.000007
iterative    0.000003
Name: seconds, dtype: float64


Data-science analyses often repeat calculations across observations, simulation trials, features, or model evaluations. An implementation that is acceptable for one small example can become infeasible when the data or number of repetitions grows. Choosing an algorithm, checking its runtime and memory use, and testing equivalent implementations are therefore data-science practices, not merely programming exercises.
A summary function can accept ordinary Python data while returning statistics in a structured form. The function converts its input to a floating-point NumPy array, checks a precondition, and returns a dictionary whose named fields are convenient in prose and tables.

def summarize(values: list[float] | np.ndarray) -&gt; dict[str, int | float]:
    """Return the count, mean, and sample standard deviation of values."""
    data = np.asarray(values, dtype=float)
    if data.ndim != 1 or data.size &lt; 2:
        raise ValueError("values must be a one-dimensional sequence of length 2 or more")
    return {
        "n": int(data.size),
        "mean": float(data.mean()),
        "sd": float(data.std(ddof=1)),
    }

summary = summarize([82, 91, 87, 76, 94])
summary

{'n': 5, 'mean': 86.0, 'sd': 7.176350047203662}


The conversion inside summarize creates an array suited to numerical work. The function therefore accepts an ordinary list but does not force every caller to learn NumPy’s input rules. Its explicit ValueError gives a useful message at the boundary of the function, instead of allowing a mysterious result later in the analysis.
Run a small, direct check whenever you write a function. This is not a complete test suite, but it records a known case and catches an accidental change to the function’s behavior:

expected_mean = 86.0
assert summarize([82, 91, 87, 76, 94])["mean"] == expected_mean

Use a function when a task recurs, when its name clarifies a multi-step calculation, or when you want to test one part of an analysis independently. Do not turn every one-line expression into a function; an unnecessary function can hide a simple calculation.
